#
# Video calling off, because nothing on this system can carry it, and leaving
# it on costs a busy loop in the radio daemon.
#
# The stock vendor declares persist.vendor.vilte_support=1 and
# persist.vendor.viwifi_support=1. Video over LTE and over Wi-Fi on MediaTek
# needs two halves: vtservice_hidl in the vendor image, which is here and
# running, and the system-side vtservice daemon, which lived in the stock MTK
# system image and has no counterpart in LineageOS. The radio daemon does not
# know that. Profiled with simpleperf, its RfxSender_11 thread spends all its
# time in
#
#   RmcVtReqHandler::handleRequestUpdateOpid
#     RmcVtReqHandler::sendMsgToVTS
#       usleep
#
# retrying a message to a video service that never answers. Measured: about
# 4800 wakeups a second and 7.3 percent of a core, around the clock, whether
# a SIM is inserted or not -- the single largest idle drain found on this
# phone. With both properties at 0 the daemon drops to about 6 wakeups a
# second and 0.35 percent of a core.
#
# Voice over LTE is governed by other properties (see device.mk,
# persist.vendor.mtk.volte.enable) and is not affected.
#
# The value has to be forced, not just set once: vendor/build.prop defines
# both properties, and a value defined in a build.prop wins over the one saved
# in /data at every boot. post-fs-data runs before class main, where the radio
# daemon (vendor.ril-daemon-mtk) is started; the property triggers catch any
# later write of 1.

on post-fs-data
    setprop persist.vendor.vilte_support 0
    setprop persist.vendor.viwifi_support 0

on property:persist.vendor.vilte_support=1
    setprop persist.vendor.vilte_support 0

on property:persist.vendor.viwifi_support=1
    setprop persist.vendor.viwifi_support 0
